WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.020
Git behavior can be influenced by configuration at multiple levels: repository, global, system, environment, and tool-specific settings.

2
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:15.880
Automation should know which configuration it depends on and which configuration it ignores.

3
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:24.300
A script that behaves differently based on a developer's global configuration or a runner's inherited environment can be difficult to review and reproduce.

4
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:31.120
Running unreviewed repository content in privileged automation can expose secrets or deployment authority.

5
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.760
Hooks and related tooling also deserve attention.

6
00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:42.140
Some workflows execute scripts as part of commit, checkout, merge, build, release, or packaging steps.

7
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.680
Some build and release systems run repository-provided scripts after checkout.

8
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:59.220
That can be appropriate when the repository content has passed the expected review path, but it is risky when unreviewed content runs in privileged automation with secrets or deployment authority.

9
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:05.420
Secure design treats repository content as input until it has passed the expected trust path.

10
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.640
A checked-out file is not automatically safe to execute merely because it came from Git.

11
00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:20.340
The script should understand whether the ref is trusted, whether the workflow is allowed to run repository-provided code, and whether the runtime has sensitive credentials available.

12
00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:24.040
Low-trust content and high-privilege execution should be separated.

13
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.600
Avoid hidden behavior in privileged automation.

14
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:34.100
Document required Git configuration, tool versions, environment variables, hooks, and execution context.

15
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.360
Review them like other deployment assumptions.

16
00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:43.340
If automation depends on a specific config value or intentionally disables a behavior, that decision should be visible.

17
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:49.040
Predictable Git automation is safer because reviewers can see what will run and under which assumptions.
